00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:02.090 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  this is your Tech news briefing for  Thursday October 20th I'm Zoe Thomas for  The Wall Street Journal  after back-to-back quarters of  subscriber losses Netflix appears to be  turning things around on Tuesday it said  it added 2.4 million subscribers more  than double what it had forecast  as Netflix battles growing competition  and streaming a space it once easily  dominated  it's been testing out new ways to  increase visits to its site boost  revenue and of course get new members to  sign up joining us to discuss all of  that and where Netflix goes next is our  Netflix and streaming reporter Sarah  Krauss hi Sarah thanks for joining me  thanks for having me so Sarah Netflix  subscriber growth for the third quarter  was surprising to a lot of people what  do we know about how it pulled off that  boost so Netflix added 2.4 million new  subscribers in the September quarter and  they had said to Wall Street that they  were likely to gain a million customers  Netflix shares were up about 14 as of  Wednesday morning which sort of reflects  the jump that happened in after hours  trading after the earnings report they  were helped During the period by  releases of popular shows like Cobra Kai  they had some high profile movies  released like the gray man which is one  of their films where they're trying to  sort of really do some franchise  building which Netflix hasn't really had  in the past as well as some surprise  wins like purple hearts which is not you  know like the flashiest of movies but  did do very well among its viewers and  then finally towards the end of the  quarter the Jeffrey Dahmer story has  done incredibly well and shot up to the  top of its most watched TV charts so the  combination of that really strong  content sort of lifted them during the  period so Netflix saw the subscriber  growth where did it come from  the vast majority of the subscriber  growth for Netflix during the third  quarter was from Asia they did add  customers in each region but Asia was  really the sort of outstanding region in  terms of net new subscribers another  interesting sort of change during the  quarter was that for the first time in  Netflix's history the number of  subscribers in Europe the Middle East  and Africa surpassed the customer base  in the US and Canada now the U.S and  Canada is still the largest market by  Revenue but it's interesting to see as  Netflix makes that International push  where that growth is coming from and is  that growth also translating to the kind  of content that they have on the  platform it is I mean one of the things  that you've seen squid game was sort of  an early example of it of content that  started in Korea and then really spread  like wildfire and took off around the  world in the third quarter one of the  sort of strengths that the company  called out was extraordinary attorney Wu  which is another Korean title but that  did well globally there was another film  from Denmark that also did really well  and so Netflix definitely played up the  idea that you know hey we're making hits  in other parts of the world that are  appealing to viewers and all different  sorts of markets that having a hit in  one country or region doesn't mean that  that content is going to stay there so  there's been this focus on content but  you know they've also been looking at  other strategies to get subscribers and  to keep subscribers one of them is this  ad supported tier we've mentioned it a  few times on the show where do things  stand with that so Netflix is in the  midst of two really important strategic  shifts that are pretty fundamental to  its fortunes going forward the first is  the ad tier is you say and Netflix the  week prior to its earnings report made  more sort of concrete announcements  around its ad tier so it said that the  plan will roll out in early November and  that it will cost 6.99 a month so having  that Clarity around the pricing and the  expected launch date I think gave  investors a lot of confidence another  thing to note about both the price point  and the timing is that it's a dollar  less per month than what Disney will  charge for its ad tier starting in  December and the launch is a month  before Disney so maybe trying to  undercut some of the competition what's  the other way that they're looking to  boost Revenue the other important  strategic shift is cracking down on  password sharing so one of the things  that helped Netflix as it grew was that  members sort of widely shared their  accounts and Netflix kind of celebrated  it for a while too because it was  visibility for the platform people  talked about what they were watching  with friends and family they share their  password but now we're in a whole  different sort of streaming landscape  where competition is such that Netflix  needs to make sure that it's maximizing  the money it makes from each customer  and so they've said that early next year  they're going to ask households that  share accounts outside of the bounds of  what their plans allow to pay a little  bit more to do so what is Netflix doing  to keep track of subscriber movement  whether they're dropping off the  platform or you know staying but maybe  not using it as much how are they  keeping track of users activity so  Netflix has a number of internal metrics  that help it keep an eye on the quality  of content and the success of the  content Investments that they're making  as well as the product improvements and  viewers overall engagement with the  platform so a really important metric  for Netflix lately in particular is not  only how sticky content is so what  percent of people who start watching a  title make it through 75 minutes or more  in the first month basically but also  the number of times per month that  members are coming back to the platform  and that's something that really matters  as Netflix is trying to keep members  engaged at a time when streaming  consumers generally have become more  fickle and are more willing to turn off  Services when they're not active using  it at the same time Netflix is trying to  understand how people are streaming now  that they're going back to the office  and traveling more and doing more  activities outside of the home you know  Sarah it feels like even though we're  talking about Revenue even though we're  talking about subscribers we keep coming  back to content and you know analysts  we've heard over and over again say  content is King and even with growing  competition in the streaming space if  you have good content your platform will  be fine but we are seeing so much more  competition in this space so does that  still hold true content is the  differentiator still and as you say it's  a hard thing to win on because it takes  a lot of really smart bets and smart  dollars spent and it's the thing that  these platforms now there's about a  dozen mainstream streaming Platforms in  the US they're all competing on the same  thing so you know Netflix said that it  feels like its pipeline is really strong  and that they've gotten better at sort  of smoothing out releases such that they  have a steady March or steady drum beat  of hits that are likely to create Buzz  at that in turn drives fresh sort of  subscribers to Netflix or that keeps its  current subscriber base really excited  all right that's our Netflix and  streaming reporter Sarah Krauss thanks  so much for explaining this for us Sarah  thank you and that's it for today's Tech  news briefing if you want more Tech  stories check out our website wsj.com  I'm Zoe Thomas for The Wall Street  Journal thanks for listening  [Music]  thank you

